ella it's not just an issue for poorer  nations is it if you look at what's  happening in canada right now you look  to the us during hurricane season the  australian wildfires what we saw in  europe  over the summer those extreme weather  events causing internal displacement  is it surprising then that there isn't  more urgency about this more urgent  action being taken  when we put it like that maybe it's  surprising but the the fact of the  matter is that if it doesn't affect us  directly or we don't think it affects us  directly we're more um likely to to  ignore it and pretend it's not an issue  the thing is it's going to affect all of  us it already affects all of us climate  impacts are being felt  here in the uk they're being felt in  canada they're being felt in the usa  they're being felt in the developing and  developed world and the fact is it's  going to get worse especially if we do  the little we're doing already to adapt  and to mitigate climate change the more  warming we see in the future the more  devastating these impacts are going to  become and we absolutely have to do  everything that we can to make sure that  the worst impacts of climate change are  avoided  and what about those climate finance and  adaptation pledges made at cop 26 then  anna are they going to make any  difference  of course every step makes a difference  and i think the acknowledgement that the  issues of adaptation loss and damage  just transition have had at cop26 are  steps forward but you know so far mostly  its words uh so far  we have not seen anywhere near the  amount of financing that the most  effective country affected countries are  actually requesting  and the other thing is um one of the  things that you know i was in in the  global trade union delegation at cop26  and one of the things that we were  really calling for is that climate  finance that money that rich countries  put in to help poorer countries adapt or  mitigate  climate change  that those funds uh